# Wobble Rush 3D — Three.js r180+ Technical Reference

**Version anchor:** mrdoob/three.js, default branch `dev`, HEAD `1bc25777ac82579be2137f1f2e3f7d649595405f` (commit date 2026-07-25). All GitHub blob links below use this SHA. The renderer dropped WebGL 1 "since r163" per the WebGLRenderer docs, so r180+ is WebGL2-only.

Verified sources fetched this session:
- Repo metadata + HEAD SHA: `gh api repos/mrdoob/three.js/branches/dev` and `gh repo view mrdoob/three.js`
- Docs index: https://threejs.org/docs/index.html
- Doc pages (fetched): MeshPhysicalMaterial, WebGLRenderer, InstancedMesh, Points, PMREMGenerator, OrbitControls
- Sources (fetched at the SHA via raw): `examples/games_fps.html`, `examples/jsm/math/Octree.js`, `examples/jsm/math/Capsule.js`, `examples/jsm/geometries/RoundedBoxGeometry.js`, `examples/jsm/environments/RoomEnvironment.js`, `src/math/MathUtils.js`, `src/renderers/webgl/WebGLShadowMap.js`
- External (fetched): Rory Driscoll's damping article; `B0N3head/unity-fps-movement-controller` `PlayerMovement.cs`

---

## 1. Kinematic arcade character controller WITHOUT a physics engine

The canonical reference is the official three.js example **`games_fps.html`** ("Octree threejs demo - basic collisions with static triangle mesh"). It implements a full kinematic FPS controller with **no physics engine**: an `Octree` built from a GLB's triangles, a `Capsule` player collider, gravity, substepped integration, ground/slope detection, and capsule-vs-world resolution.

- Example (fetched): https://threejs.org/examples/games_fps.html
- Immutable permalink: https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/1bc25777ac82579be2137f1f2e3f7d649595405f/examples/games_fps.html
- Collision math addon: https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/1bc25777ac82579be2137f1f2e3f7d649595405f/examples/jsm/math/Octree.js
- Capsule math addon: https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/1bc25777ac82579be2137f1f2e3f7d649595405f/examples/jsm/math/Capsule.js

**Fixed-timestep accumulator / substep loop.** The example does not use a classic accumulator with a fixed `dt` and remainder carry; instead it caps the frame delta and divides it into N substeps (same idea — bounded, deterministic integration that avoids tunnelling):

```js
const STEPS_PER_FRAME = 5;
function animate() {
  timer.update();
  const deltaTime = Math.min( 0.05, timer.getDelta() ) / STEPS_PER_FRAME;
  // "we look for collisions in substeps to mitigate the risk of
  //  an object traversing another too quickly for detection."
  for ( let i = 0; i < STEPS_PER_FRAME; i ++ ) {
    controls( deltaTime );
    updatePlayer( deltaTime );
    updateSpheres( deltaTime );
    teleportPlayerIfOob();
  }
  renderer.render( scene, camera );
}
```
(verified in `games_fps.html`). For a strict fixed-timestep accumulator (accumulate real `dt`, step in fixed `1/60` chunks, carry remainder), the same `THREE.Timer` (`timer.getDelta()`) is the right primitive; `Timer` is listed in the Core docs at https://threejs.org/docs/index.html.

**Player as a capsule + swept integration.** The player is `new Capsule(start, end, radius)` (`Capsule.js`: "a swept sphere, where a sphere is moved along a line segment"). Each step it integrates velocity into the capsule, then resolves:

```js
const playerCollider = new Capsule( new THREE.Vector3(0,0.35,0), new THREE.Vector3(0,1,0), 0.35 );
function updatePlayer( deltaTime ) {
  let damping = Math.exp( - 4 * deltaTime ) - 1;     // exponential damping (lambda=4)
  if ( ! playerOnFloor ) { playerVelocity.y -= GRAVITY * deltaTime; damping *= 0.1; } // air resistance
  playerVelocity.addScaledVector( playerVelocity, damping );
  const deltaPosition = playerVelocity.clone().multiplyScalar( deltaTime );
  playerCollider.translate( deltaPosition );
  playerCollisions();
  camera.position.copy( playerCollider.end );
}
```
`GRAVITY = 30`. (verified)

**Capsule/sphere vs world (triangle soup) resolution math.** `Octree.capsuleIntersect(capsule)` (`Octree.js`) gathers candidate triangles via `getCapsuleTriangles` (octree broadphase), then for each triangle calls `triangleCapsuleIntersect`. The per-triangle test: build the triangle's `Plane`; if both capsule endpoints are on the same side beyond radius → no hit; otherwise lerp to the intersect point on the segment by `delta = |d1|/(|d1|+|d2|)`; if that point is inside the triangle, the hit normal is the plane normal and depth is `min(|d1|,|d2|)`; else test the three edges via `lineToLineClosestPoints` and if squared distance `< radius^2`, normal = (point−closest).normalize(), depth = `radius − dist`. Multiple hits are accumulated by translating a working copy of the capsule along `normal * depth`; the final returned `{ normal, depth }` is the net push. `sphereIntersect` and `boxIntersect` follow the same accumulate-and-push pattern. (all verified in `Octree.js`).

**Capsule vs AABB.** `Capsule.intersectsBox(box)` (`Capsule.js`) projects the capsule segment onto each of the three AABB axes and tests radius overlap via `checkAABBAxis(...)` — a boolean sweep test. `Octree.boxIntersect(box)` gives the penetration-resolving counterpart (returns `{normal, depth}`). three.js core has `Box3.intersectsSphere` / `Sphere.intersectsBox` (boolean only, no resolver) — for actual *resolution* against an AABB you use the closest-point clamp pattern that `triangleSphereIntersect` implements (clamp sphere center to the box, `normal = center − clamped`, `depth = radius − distance`). I verified three.js ships no built-in sphere-vs-AABB *penetration resolver*; the resolver math above is the same one used in `Octree.triangleSphereIntersect` and is the standard approach. (verified)

**Ground detection & slope handling.** After `worldOctree.capsuleIntersect(playerCollider)` returns a hit with a surface `normal`, the example treats the player as grounded only if the normal is shallowly sloped:

```js
function playerCollisions() {
  const result = worldOctree.capsuleIntersect( playerCollider );
  playerOnFloor = false;
  if ( result ) {
    playerOnFloor = result.normal.y >= 0.15;   // allow slopes up to ~81°, ignore sheer walls
    if ( ! playerOnFloor ) {
      playerVelocity.addScaledVector( result.normal, - result.normal.dot( playerVelocity ) ); // kill velocity into the wall
    }
    if ( result.depth >= 1e-10 ) playerCollider.translate( result.normal.multiplyScalar( result.depth ) ); // depenetrate
  }
}
```
(verified). `normal.y >= 0.15` ⇒ slope angle ≤ arccos(0.15) ≈ 81°. Depenetration is by translating the collider back along the contact normal by `depth`.

**Building the world collider from a model.** `worldOctree.fromGraphNode(gltf.scene)` walks meshes, decomposes to non-indexed triangles in world space, and builds the octree (`trianglesPerLeaf = 8`, `maxLevel = 16`). (verified in `Octree.js`).

