## 2. Game feel for platformers (coyote time, jump buffer, variable jump, air control, accel/friction)

**Air control, acceleration, jump impulse — numeric values from a real three.js example.** From `games_fps.html` (verified):

```js
const GRAVITY = 30;
// in controls(deltaTime):
const speedDelta = deltaTime * ( playerOnFloor ? 25 : 8 ); // ground accel 25, air accel 8 (air control)
if ( keyStates[ 'KeyW' ] ) playerVelocity.add( getForwardVector().multiplyScalar( speedDelta ) );
...
if ( playerOnFloor && keyStates[ 'Space' ] ) playerVelocity.y = 15; // jump impulse
```
Damping (friction) is the exponential-decay form `Math.exp( -4 * deltaTime ) - 1` (λ = 4) on the ground and `× 0.1` in the air. These are concrete, citable numbers from an official three.js example: gravity 30, jump velocity 15, ground accel 25, air accel 8, damping λ = 4, slope tolerance `normal.y ≥ 0.15`.

**Coyote time + jump buffering + variable (hold-to-rise) jump height — numeric values from an open-source controller.** `B0N3head/unity-fps-movement-controller` `PlayerMovement.cs` (fetched): https://github.com/B0N3head/unity-fps-movement-controller/blob/main/PlayerMovement.cs

Verified tunables and the matching logic:
```csharp
public float coyoteTime = 0.2f;   // "How long you have to jump after leaving a ledge (seconds)"
public float jumpBuffer  = 0.1f;  // "How long I should buffer your jump input for (seconds)"
public float jumpForce   = 110f;  // initial jump impulse
public float jumpAccel   = 10f;   // continuous jump force while held
public float jumpTime    = 0.4f;  // max jump-up time (variable jump height window)
public float extraGravity = 0.1f; // fall quicker (faster fall than rise)
```
Logic (verified in the same file):
- Coyote: `if (areWeGrounded) coyoteTimeCounter = coyoteTime; else coyoteTimeCounter -= Time.deltaTime;`
- Buffer: `if (wantingToJump) jumpBufferCounter = jumpBuffer; else jumpBufferCounter -= Time.deltaTime;`
- Jump fires when `coyoteTimeCounter > 0 && jumpBufferCounter > 0` (both windows overlap).
- Variable jump height (short hop vs full): initial `AddForce(up * jumpForce, Impulse)`, then while the button is held and `endJumpTime > Time.time` (the `jumpTime = 0.4s` window), `AddForce(up * jumpAccel, Acceleration)` continues — releasing early cuts the jump short. `extraGravity` makes the fall snappier than the rise (classic platformer asymmetry).

This single verified file covers coyote time (0.2 s), jump buffering (0.1 s), and variable jump height (0.4 s sustain window) with real numbers.

**Frame-rate-independent damping / accel-friction curves.** The exponential-damping idiom used above (`Math.exp(-λ·dt)`) is exactly `THREE.MathUtils.damp`:

```js
// src/math/MathUtils.js (fetched)
function damp( x, y, lambda, dt ) {
  return lerp( x, y, 1 - Math.exp( - lambda * dt ) );
}
```
Permalink: https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/1bc25777ac82579be2137f1f2e3f7d649595405f/src/math/MathUtils.js — and the doc links it to the canonical article. The math is derived in Rory Driscoll, "Frame Rate Independent Damping using Lerp" (fetched, full text): https://www.rorydriscoll.com/2016/03/07/frame-rate-independent-damping-using-lerp/ — which shows `a(t+n) = Lerp(a, b, 1 - Pow(r, n))` is equivalent to exponential decay `Lerp(a, b, 1 - Exp(-λ·n))` with `λ = -ln(r)`. Use this for both velocity smoothing (λ ≈ 4, as in the fps example) and camera follow.

**Corroboration that coyote/buffer are a widespread pattern:** a `gh search code coyoteTime` returned multiple independent implementations (e.g. `TerryCavanagh/triangle-run` `scripts/Player.gd` has `var coyotetime = 0;` and `jumpqueued = 0` — a jump-queue/buffer). I verified these via the GitHub code-search response but did **not** fetch a specific blob permalink for `triangle-run`, so I'm citing it only as search corroboration, not as a quoted source.

**GDC talks:** the well-known "Math for Game Programmers: Building a Better Jump" (GDC) is frequently cited for deriving gravity/initial-velocity from desired apex time and jump height. I could **not** fetch/verify a stable GDC Vault URL through the read-only tools available (GDC Vault is largely behind auth), so I am deliberately **not** providing a permalink for it. The jump-math approach (pick apex time `t_apex` and height `h`, then `g = 2h/t_apex²`, `v0 = 2h/t_apex`) is the same exponential/parabolic model underlying the values above; treat the GDC attribution as unverified.

