## 3. Third-person follow camera (spring/damped lerp, look-ahead, collision-free offset)

**Damped/spring follow primitive (verified).** `THREE.MathUtils.damp(x, y, lambda, dt)` is the frame-rate-independent damped lerp to use for a follow camera — apply it per-axis to `camera.position` toward a desired offset position:

```js
camera.position.x = THREE.MathUtils.damp( camera.position.x, desired.x, lambda, dt );
camera.position.y = THREE.MathUtils.damp( camera.position.y, desired.y, lambda, dt );
camera.position.z = THREE.MathUtils.damp( camera.position.z, desired.z, lambda, dt );
```
- Source: https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/1bc25777ac82579be2137f1f2e3f7d649595405f/src/math/MathUtils.js
- Theory (fetched): https://www.rorydriscoll.com/2016/03/07/frame-rate-independent-damping-using-lerp/ — `lambda` is the decay rate; "a higher lambda value will make the movement more sudden, and a lower value will make the movement more gradual" (MathUtils doc, fetched).

**Typical damping constants (verified anchors).** 
- The fps example uses λ = 4 for *player velocity* damping (`Math.exp(-4*dt)`, verified). For a camera, a smaller λ (slower, smoother follow) is typical; three.js's own `OrbitControls` exposes `enableDamping` (default `false`) and `dampingFactor` (default `0.05`) — verified at https://threejs.org/docs/pages/OrbitControls.html. Note OrbitControls' `dampingFactor` is a *per-frame* lerp factor (the very pattern Rory Driscoll warns is frame-rate-dependent); for a follow cam prefer the `damp(...,lambda,dt)` form. I'm **flagging** that three.js ships **no built-in third-person follow camera** — only `OrbitControls`/`MapControls`/`PointerLockControls`. The fps example hard-attaches the camera to the collider (`camera.position.copy(playerCollider.end)`, verified); a damped follow is a roll-your-own using `MathUtils.damp`.

**Look-ahead and collision-free offset — caveat.** These are standard idioms (target = `playerPos + playerVelocity * lookAheadK`; camera desired = `playerPos + offsetRotatedByYaw`; optional single-ray pull-in to prevent clipping). I could **not** find an official three.js example or doc that implements a damped look-ahead follow cam with a specific numeric `lookAheadK` or offset, so I am not quoting numbers for those. The verified, citable building blocks are: `MathUtils.damp` (damped follow), `PerspectiveCamera` + `camera.rotation.order = 'YXZ'` (verified in `games_fps.html` for yaw-then-pitch), and `Raycaster` (listed in Core docs) for a collision pull-in ray. Treat specific look-ahead/offset constants as per-game tuned values, not from an official source.

---

## 4. Toy-like glossy visuals (plastic/vinyl) in Three.js

**MeshPhysicalMaterial — clearcoat + sheen for plastic/vinyl (verified defaults/ranges).** Doc (fetched): https://threejs.org/docs/pages/MeshPhysicalMaterial.html . Relevant properties and defaults:
- `.clearcoat` — intensity of the clear-coat layer, `0.0`–`1.0`, default `0`. ("car paints, carbon fiber, and wet surfaces … a clear, reflective layer on top of another layer")
- `.clearcoatRoughness` — `0.0`–`1.0`, default `0`.
- `.clearcoatNormalMap` / `.clearcoatNormalScale` (default `(1,1)`) — independent normals for the coat.
- `.sheen` — `0.0`–`1.0`, default `0`; `.sheenColor` (default `(0,0,0)`); `.sheenRoughness` (default `1`). The doc notes sheen is "for representing cloth and fabric," but a small sheen also gives vinyl/soft-plastic its edge-rim fuzz.
- `.ior` — `1.0`–`2.333`, default `1.5`; `.reflectivity` — `0.0`–`1.0`, default `0.5` (corresponds to IOR 1.5).
- The doc explicitly warns: "For best results, always specify an environment map when using this material." (i.e., you need IBL — see PMREM/RoomEnvironment below).

**Clearcoat idiom from an official example (fetched):** https://threejs.org/examples/webgl_materials_physical_clearcoat.html — car-paint sphere:
```js
let material = new THREE.MeshPhysicalMaterial( {
  clearcoat: 1.0,
  clearcoatRoughness: 0.1,
  metalness: 0.9,
  roughness: 0.5,
  color: 0x0000ff,
  normalMap: normalMap3,
  normalScale: new THREE.Vector2( 0.15, 0.15 )
} );
```
For a glossy *toy* plastic (non-metal), use `metalness: 0`, `roughness: 0.2–0.4`, `clearcoat: 1.0`, `clearcoatRoughness: 0.05–0.15`, `ior: 1.5`. Example also sets `scene.environment = texture` for IBL.

**Rounded box geometry (toy-block look) — verified.** Addon `RoundedBoxGeometry` extends `BoxGeometry`: https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/1bc25777ac82579be2137f1f2e3f7d649595405f/examples/jsm/geometries/RoundedBoxGeometry.js . Constructor: `new RoundedBoxGeometry(width=1, height=1, depth=1, segments=2, radius=0.1)`; it clamps `radius = min(width,height,depth, radius)/2` and deforms a subdivided unit box so corners/edges are rounded (with correct normals + UVs). Import: `import { RoundedBoxGeometry } from 'three/addons/geometries/RoundedBoxGeometry.js'`. For the player capsule visual, core `CapsuleGeometry` is in the built-in Geometries (verified present in the docs index at https://threejs.org/docs/index.html).

**Soft shadows — IMPORTANT r180+ correction.** The task asks for `PCFSoft`; **`PCFSoftShadowMap` has been removed in this build.** Verified in `src/renderers/webgl/WebGLShadowMap.js` (fetched):
```js
if ( this.type === PCFSoftShadowMap ) {
  warn( 'WebGLShadowMap: PCFSoftShadowMap has been removed. Using PCFShadowMap instead.' );
  this.type = PCFShadowMap;
}
```
The `WebGLRenderer` docs page (fetched, https://threejs.org/docs/pages/WebGLRenderer.html) lists `shadowMap.type` as `BasicShadowMap | PCFShadowMap | VSMShadowMap` (default `PCFShadowMap`) — `PCFSoftShadowMap` is no longer in the enum. For soft toy shadows in r180+, use **`VSMShadowMap`** (variance shadow maps, gives soft edges via a Gaussian-like blur) or `PCFShadowMap` (default). The fps example (verified) uses VSM with bias tuning:
```js
renderer.shadowMap.enabled = true;
renderer.shadowMap.type = THREE.VSMShadowMap;
// directionalLight:
directionalLight.shadow.mapSize.width = 1024; directionalLight.shadow.mapSize.height = 1024;
directionalLight.shadow.radius = 4;            // VSM blur radius
directionalLight.shadow.bias = - 0.00006;        // kill peter-panning/acne
directionalLight.shadow.camera.left = -30; ... far = 500;
```
- ShadowMap source: https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/1bc25777ac82579be2137f1f2e3f7d649595405f/src/renderers/webgl/WebGLShadowMap.js
- WebGLRenderer doc (shadowMap, toneMapping, outputColorSpace): https://threejs.org/docs/pages/WebGLRenderer.html

**ACES Filmic tone mapping + sRGB output (verified).** From the WebGLRenderer doc (fetched): `renderer.toneMapping` (default `NoToneMapping`; accepts `ACESFilmicToneMapping`, `AgXToneMapping`, `NeutralToneMapping`, etc.), `renderer.toneMappingExposure` (default `1`), `renderer.outputColorSpace` (default `SRGBColorSpace`). The clearcoat example sets:
```js
renderer.toneMapping = THREE.ACESFilmicToneMapping;
renderer.toneMappingExposure = 1.25;
```
And the fps example sets `renderer.toneMapping = THREE.ACESFilmicToneMapping`. `THREE.SRGBColorSpace` is confirmed in the global constants (docs index). For an even softer filmic look, `AgXToneMapping` is also valid in r180+ (verified in the toneMapping enum).

**Environment lighting via RoomEnvironment + PMREMGenerator (verified).** `RoomEnvironment` (addon Scene) source: https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/1bc25777ac82579be2137f1f2e3f7d649595405f/examples/jsm/environments/RoomEnvironment.js — its own docstring gives the exact idiom:
```js
const environment = new RoomEnvironment();
const pmremGenerator = new THREE.PMREMGenerator( renderer );
const envMap = pmremGenerator.fromScene( environment ).texture;
scene.environment = envMap;
```
`PMREMGenerator.fromScene(scene, sigma=0, near=0.1, far=100, {size=256})` generates a Prefiltered Mipmapped Radiance Environment map (PMREM) using GGX VNDF importance sampling (verified in the PMREMGenerator doc, https://threejs.org/docs/pages/PMREMGenerator.html). This is the cheap, no-HDR-asset way to get glossy plastic/vinyl reflections on toys. `RoomEnvironment` internally uses an `InstancedMesh` for its boxes (nice cross-reference to §6).

**Cheap stylized outlines.** Verified-available addon options (from the docs index, https://threejs.org/docs/index.html): `OutlineEffect` (Effects addon) and the postprocessing `OutlinePass` (Postprocessing addon). For a toon/toy outline, `OutlineEffect` wraps the renderer and draws back-face-extruded shells. There is also a TSL `toonOutlinePass` and `OutlineNode` (TSL section of the index) for the node-based renderer. I verified these names exist in the current docs index but did **not** fetch their full doc pages, so I'm citing them as available addons by name rather than quoting API signatures.

