## 5. Cheap GPU particle effects (Points / BufferGeometry) for landing puffs, confetti, trails

**`THREE.Points` + `BufferGeometry` is the cheap primitive (verified).** Points doc (fetched): https://threejs.org/docs/pages/Points.html — `new THREE.Points(geometry, material)`, default material `PointsMaterial`. The official dynamic-particles example (fetched) https://threejs.org/examples/webgl_points_dynamic.html shows the idiom:
```js
const geometry = new THREE.BufferGeometry();
geometry.setAttribute( 'position', positions.clone() );
geometry.setAttribute( 'initialPosition', positions.clone() );
geometry.attributes.position.setUsage( THREE.DynamicDrawUsage );   // mutable per-frame buffer
// ...
const mesh = new THREE.Points( geometry, new THREE.PointsMaterial( { size: 30, color: c } ) );
```
Per-frame the example mutates `positions` via `positions.setXYZ(i, ...)` and sets `positions.needsUpdate = true` (CPU-side update of a `DynamicDrawUsage` buffer). For landing puffs / confetti / short trails this single-`Points`-object, one-draw-call approach is the standard cheap pattern; use a circular sprite texture + `PointsMaterial.map` + `transparent:true` + `depthWrite:false` + additive blending for soft puffs.

**For fully GPU-side simulation** (no CPU per-particle loop), three.js ships `GPUComputationRenderer` (verified present in the docs index, Misc addon) which runs compute via fragment-shader ping-pong textures; you'd then render the resulting velocity/position fields with a `Points` mesh. There are also WebGPU/TSL compute-particle examples in the examples listing (`webgpu_compute_particles_*`, `webgpu_compute_particles_snow`/`_rain` — verified in the `examples/` listing I fetched), but those require `WebGPURenderer`, not `WebGLRenderer`. For a WebGL r180+ browser game, `Points` + `DynamicDrawUsage` (CPU) or `GPUComputationRenderer` (GPU) are the verified options.

Permalink for the example: https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/1bc25777ac82579be2137f1f2e3f7d649595405f/examples/webgl_points_dynamic.html

---

## 6. Performance: instancing, frustum culling, pixel-ratio clamping

**InstancedMesh for repeated obstacles (verified API).** Doc (fetched): https://threejs.org/docs/pages/InstancedMesh.html — `new THREE.InstancedMesh(geometry, material, count)`; set per-instance transforms with `setMatrixAt(index, matrix4)` and per-instance colors with `setColorAt(index, color)`, then set `instanceMatrix.needsUpdate = true` / `instanceColor.needsUpdate = true`. The doc states this "reduce[s] the number of draw calls." A verified in-tree usage is `RoomEnvironment.js`:
```js
const boxes = new InstancedMesh( geometry, boxMaterial, 6 );
const transform = new Object3D();
transform.position.set(...); transform.rotation.set(...); transform.scale.set(...);
transform.updateMatrix();
boxes.setMatrixAt( 0, transform.matrix );
```
Permalink: https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/1bc25777ac82579be2137f1f2e3f7d649595405f/examples/jsm/environments/RoomEnvironment.js

**Frustum culling (verified, automatic + opt-out).** The renderer culls objects whose `Object3D.frustumCulled` is `true` (default) using the object's bounding sphere against the camera frustum; the shadow pass uses exactly this: `if ( ( object.castShadow || ... ) && ( ! object.frustumCulled || object.intersectsFrustum( _frustum ) ) )` (verified in `WebGLShadowMap.js`, https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js/blob/1bc25777ac82579be2137f1f2e3f7d649595405f/src/renderers/webgl/WebGLShadowMap.js ). For `InstancedMesh`, the doc notes the bounding sphere is "automatically computed … when it is needed, e.g., for ray casting or view frustum culling," and `computeBoundingSphere()` must be called by your app after `setMatrixAt` changes (verified in the InstancedMesh doc). So: keep `frustumCulled = true` (default), and call `instancedMesh.computeBoundingSphere()` whenever you move instances, or the whole `InstancedMesh` may be wrongly culled or never culled.

**Pixel-ratio clamping (verified API; clamp value flagged as convention).** `renderer.setPixelRatio(value)` (verified, https://threejs.org/docs/pages/WebGLRenderer.html ) sets the device pixel ratio and resizes the canvas. The official fps and clearcoat examples call `renderer.setPixelRatio( window.devicePixelRatio )` *unclamped* (verified) — on a 3× DPR phone this triples the fragment count. The widely-used guard is `renderer.setPixelRatio( Math.min( window.devicePixelRatio, 2 ) )`. **Caveat:** I could not find an official three.js doc page that prescribes the `min(…, 2)` clamp value, so I'm citing the `setPixelRatio` API (verified) and flagging the specific `2` cap as community convention rather than an official recommendation.

**Additional verified perf notes:**
- `WebGLRenderer.info.render.calls / triangles / points` (verified in the WebGLRenderer doc) gives you live draw-call/triangle counters to verify instancing actually reduced calls.
- `WebGLRenderer.compile(scene, camera)` / `compileAsync` (verified) lets you precompile shaders to avoid first-frame hitches.
- `InstancedMesh` is one draw call for `count` instances; if you also need many *different* geometries batched, the docs index lists `BatchedMesh` (Objects) as the newer batching alternative (verified to exist; I did not fetch its doc page).

